            <div xml:id="d582">

                <pb facs="0853" n="739" xml:id="pg739"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">724.3415/1250a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Chargé in
                        Bolivia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Trueblood</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-06-30T17:00:00-04:00">June 30, 1931—5
                    p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>27. The Secretary of Bolivian Legation just called at Department on behalf of the
                    Minister to say that instructions have been sent to the Bolivian Minister in
                    Asuncion to leave. Apparently the second Bolivian note is not to be delivered.
                    The Department felt that the matter was pretty well worked out so that the
                    Paraguayan reply to the second Bolivian note would be conciliatory and offer a
                    satisfactory solution. This makes it impossible. Please find out whether
                    instructions have been sent and whether it would not be possible to hold them up
                    or send immediately other instructions to the Minister not to act as yet. The
                    neutral note has not been answered nor has the Bolivian Government offered any
                    suggestion to any of the neutrals as to how they could be helpful in
                    straightening out this matter. If Bolivia will withhold action and discuss the
                    matter frankly with you and other neutrals it seems certain that a satisfactory
                    adjustment can be arrived at. Report by cable.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Castle</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
